{"archive_version": "20260604.144757", "book_slug": "chemistry-atoms-first", "book_state": "retired", "book_title": "Chemistry: Atoms First", "book_uuid": "4539ae23-1ccc-421e-9b25-843acbb6c4b0", "book_version": "741a7af", "classes": ["prose_chem_script"], "content_url": "https://openstax.org/apps/archive/20260604.144757/contents/4539ae23-1ccc-421e-9b25-843acbb6c4b0@741a7af:15b0d34d-3a2d-495e-a477-8f947338866f.json", "element_id": "fs-idm17485520", "id": "openstax/chemistry-atoms-first/18-12-occurrence-preparation-and-properties-of-the-noble-gases/sc-000", "index_on_page": 0, "kind": "script", "license_name": "Creative Commons Attribution License", "license_url": "http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/", "page_slug": "18-12-occurrence-preparation-and-properties-of-the-noble-gases", "page_title": "18.12 Occurrence, Preparation, and Properties of the Noble Gases", "page_uuid": "15b0d34d-3a2d-495e-a477-8f947338866f", "retrieved_at": "2026-10-14", "runs": [{"after": "_{2},", "before": " forms a difluoride, KrF", "class": "prose_chem_script", "script": "sub", "value": "2"}], "source": "openstax", "source_url": "https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-atoms-first/pages/18-12-occurrence-preparation-and-properties-of-the-noble-gases#fs-idm17485520", "text": "Krypton-xenon flash tubes are used to take high-speed photographs. An electric discharge through such a tube gives a very intense light that lasts only [math] of a second. Krypton forms a difluoride, KrF_{2}, which is thermally unstable at room temperature."}
{"archive_version": "20260604.144757", "book_slug": "chemistry-atoms-first", "book_state": "retired", "book_title": "Chemistry: Atoms First", "book_uuid": "4539ae23-1ccc-421e-9b25-843acbb6c4b0", "book_version": "741a7af", "classes": ["prose_chem_script"], "content_url": "https://openstax.org/apps/archive/20260604.144757/contents/4539ae23-1ccc-421e-9b25-843acbb6c4b0@741a7af:15b0d34d-3a2d-495e-a477-8f947338866f.json", "element_id": "fs-idm82366128", "id": "openstax/chemistry-atoms-first/18-12-occurrence-preparation-and-properties-of-the-noble-gases/sc-001", "index_on_page": 1, "kind": "script", "license_name": "Creative Commons Attribution License", "license_url": "http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/", "page_slug": "18-12-occurrence-preparation-and-properties-of-the-noble-gases", "page_title": "18.12 Occurrence, Preparation, and Properties of the Noble Gases", "page_uuid": "15b0d34d-3a2d-495e-a477-8f947338866f", "retrieved_at": "2026-10-14", "runs": [{"after": "_{2},", "before": "e. Xenon difluoride, XeF", "class": "prose_chem_script", "script": "sub", "value": "2"}, {"after": "_{4},", "before": "Xenon tetrafluoride, XeF", "class": "prose_chem_script", "script": "sub", "value": "4"}, {"after": "_{6},", "before": " xenon hexafluoride, XeF", "class": "prose_chem_script", "script": "sub", "value": "6"}], "source": "openstax", "source_url": "https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-atoms-first/pages/18-12-occurrence-preparation-and-properties-of-the-noble-gases#fs-idm82366128", "text": "Stable compounds of xenon form when xenon reacts with fluorine. Xenon difluoride, XeF_{2}, forms after heating an excess of xenon gas with fluorine gas and then cooling. The material forms colorless crystals, which are stable at room temperature in a dry atmosphere. Xenon tetrafluoride, XeF_{4}, and xenon hexafluoride, XeF_{6}, are prepared in an analogous manner, with a stoichiometric amount of fluorine and an excess of fluorine, respectively. Compounds with oxygen are prepared by replacing fluorine atoms in the xenon fluorides with oxygen."}
{"archive_version": "20260604.144757", "book_slug": "chemistry-atoms-first", "book_state": "retired", "book_title": "Chemistry: Atoms First", "book_uuid": "4539ae23-1ccc-421e-9b25-843acbb6c4b0", "book_version": "741a7af", "classes": ["prose_chem_script"], "content_url": "https://openstax.org/apps/archive/20260604.144757/contents/4539ae23-1ccc-421e-9b25-843acbb6c4b0@741a7af:15b0d34d-3a2d-495e-a477-8f947338866f.json", "element_id": "fs-idp93560064", "id": "openstax/chemistry-atoms-first/18-12-occurrence-preparation-and-properties-of-the-noble-gases/sc-002", "index_on_page": 2, "kind": "script", "license_name": "Creative Commons Attribution License", "license_url": "http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/", "page_slug": "18-12-occurrence-preparation-and-properties-of-the-noble-gases", "page_title": "18.12 Occurrence, Preparation, and Properties of the Noble Gases", "page_uuid": "15b0d34d-3a2d-495e-a477-8f947338866f", "retrieved_at": "2026-10-14", "runs": [{"after": "_{6}", "before": "When XeF", "class": "prose_chem_script", "script": "sub", "value": "6"}, {"after": "_{3}", "before": "water, a solution of XeO", "class": "prose_chem_script", "script": "sub", "value": "3"}], "source": "openstax", "source_url": "https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-atoms-first/pages/18-12-occurrence-preparation-and-properties-of-the-noble-gases#fs-idp93560064", "text": "When XeF_{6} reacts with water, a solution of XeO_{3} results and the xenon remains in the 6+-oxidation state:"}
{"archive_version": "20260604.144757", "book_slug": "chemistry-atoms-first", "book_state": "retired", "book_title": "Chemistry: Atoms First", "book_uuid": "4539ae23-1ccc-421e-9b25-843acbb6c4b0", "book_version": "741a7af", "classes": ["prose_chem_script"], "content_url": "https://openstax.org/apps/archive/20260604.144757/contents/4539ae23-1ccc-421e-9b25-843acbb6c4b0@741a7af:15b0d34d-3a2d-495e-a477-8f947338866f.json", "element_id": "fs-idm5757248", "id": "openstax/chemistry-atoms-first/18-12-occurrence-preparation-and-properties-of-the-noble-gases/sc-003", "index_on_page": 3, "kind": "script", "license_name": "Creative Commons Attribution License", "license_url": "http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/", "page_slug": "18-12-occurrence-preparation-and-properties-of-the-noble-gases", "page_title": "18.12 Occurrence, Preparation, and Properties of the Noble Gases", "page_uuid": "15b0d34d-3a2d-495e-a477-8f947338866f", "retrieved_at": "2026-10-14", "runs": [{"after": "_{3},", "before": "olid xenon trioxide, XeO", "class": "prose_chem_script", "script": "sub", "value": "3"}, {"after": "_{6}", "before": "ously detonate. Both XeF", "class": "prose_chem_script", "script": "sub", "value": "6"}, {"after": "_{3}", "before": "te. Both XeF_{6} and XeO", "class": "prose_chem_script", "script": "sub", "value": "3"}], "source": "openstax", "source_url": "https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-atoms-first/pages/18-12-occurrence-preparation-and-properties-of-the-noble-gases#fs-idm5757248", "text": "Dry, solid xenon trioxide, XeO_{3}, is extremely explosive—it will spontaneously detonate. Both XeF_{6} and XeO_{3} disproportionate in basic solution, producing xenon, oxygen, and salts of the perxenate ion, [math] in which xenon reaches its maximum oxidation sate of 8+."}
{"archive_version": "20260604.144757", "book_slug": "chemistry-atoms-first", "book_state": "retired", "book_title": "Chemistry: Atoms First", "book_uuid": "4539ae23-1ccc-421e-9b25-843acbb6c4b0", "book_version": "741a7af", "classes": ["prose_chem_script"], "content_url": "https://openstax.org/apps/archive/20260604.144757/contents/4539ae23-1ccc-421e-9b25-843acbb6c4b0@741a7af:15b0d34d-3a2d-495e-a477-8f947338866f.json", "element_id": "fs-idm96517872", "id": "openstax/chemistry-atoms-first/18-12-occurrence-preparation-and-properties-of-the-noble-gases/sc-004", "index_on_page": 4, "kind": "script", "license_name": "Creative Commons Attribution License", "license_url": "http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/", "page_slug": "18-12-occurrence-preparation-and-properties-of-the-noble-gases", "page_title": "18.12 Occurrence, Preparation, and Properties of the Noble Gases", "page_uuid": "15b0d34d-3a2d-495e-a477-8f947338866f", "retrieved_at": "2026-10-14", "runs": [{"after": "_{2}—evidence", "before": "don apparently forms RnF", "class": "prose_chem_script", "script": "sub", "value": "2"}], "source": "openstax", "source_url": "https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-atoms-first/pages/18-12-occurrence-preparation-and-properties-of-the-noble-gases#fs-idm96517872", "text": "Radon apparently forms RnF_{2}—evidence of this compound comes from radiochemical tracer techniques."}
